===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*-trom}}
From {{inh|en|enm|septre}}, {{m|enm|sceptre}}, from {{der|en|fro|sceptre}}, from {{der|en|la|scēptrum}}, from {{der|en|grc|σκῆπτρον||staff, stick, baton}}, from {{m|grc|σκήπτω||to prop, to support, to lean upon a staff}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈsɛptə/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈsɛptɚ/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-sceptre.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|British spelling}} An [[ornamental]] [[staff]] held by a [[ruling]] [[monarch]] as a [[symbol]] of [[power]].
#: {{syn|en|golden wand|royal wand}}
#* {{RQ:Cowper Homer|volume=I|book=I|page=3|passage=To the fleet he came / Bearing rich ranſom glorious to redeem / His daughter, and his hands  charged with the wreath / And golden '''ſceptre''' of the God shaft-arm’d.}}
#* {{RQ:Wilde Pomegranates|page=6|passage=But what had occupied him most was the robe he was to wear at his coronation, the robe of tissued gold, and the ruby-studded crown, and the '''sceptre''' with its rows and rings of pearls.}}

